#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Verify registry/skills.yml against the producer bodies it claims (.github#247, ADR-0017).

`skills.yml` declares, per row, a `source:` (the owning repo's canonical SKILL.md) and the
`sha256:` it was reconciled from. Its header says the rows are "GENERATED / RECONCILED FROM THE
PRODUCER MANIFESTS -- never hand-authored bytes", and every changelog entry restates the invariant
**registry = manifest = bytes**.

Nothing enforced it. Each producer repo drift-guards its OWN manifest against its OWN bodies
(FS.GG.SDD `registry skill-manifest --check`, FS.GG.Rendering / FS.GG.Game
`generate-skill-manifest.fsx --check`), but no check tied a registry row to the remote body it
names. A producer could merge a body change and this registry silently went stale -- which is
exactly what happened to `fs-gg-audio` (ADR-0024 step 4), to `fs-gg-game-core` and
`fs-gg-model-swap` before it (registry changelog 2026-07-06), and to 14 of 32 rows by the time
this check was written.

Checks, all over local checkouts (no network -- CI checks out the producer repos).

Over each row the registry ALREADY declares:

  1. SOURCE EXISTS   every row's `source:` resolves to a file in its owning repo. A renamed or
                     relocated skill currently goes unnoticed.
  2. DIGEST MATCHES  sha256(canonical body) == the declared `sha256:`.
  3. FROZEN MIRROR   for a row owned by `fs-gg-game`, FS.GG.Rendering's frozen copy at
                     `template/product-skills/<id>/SKILL.md` -- the accepted two-copies cost
                     (ADR-0022 §6, retired at P6) -- is byte-identical to the canonical body.
                     Derived, not hardcoded: skipped when Rendering has no such copy.

Over the SET of rows -- the converse direction, and the reason this file grew (.github#289, epic
#266 "coherence gates that fail open"). Checks 1-3 are each a predicate over a row that is already
declared, so a shipped skill simply ABSENT from the catalog was not a finding, it was nothing. Four
of them were: `fs-gg-collision`, `fs-gg-grids`, `fs-gg-line-drawing`, `fs-gg-visibility` -- real,
digest-matching, emitted by `--profile game`, and invisible to the gate guarding the registry
ADR-0017 calls the "single authoritative catalog". This is ADR-0014's presence-vs-content lesson
recurring in the opposite direction: we validated the CONTENT OF WHAT IS LISTED and never the
COMPLETENESS OF THE LIST.

  4. MANIFEST FOUND  every producer named by a `source:` prefix has a readable, well-shaped
                     manifest. A manifest that vanished would silently drop every skill it
                     declares out of check 5 -- the same fail-open, one level up. Fails closed.
  5. DECLARED COMPLETENESS
                     every skill any producer manifest declares has a row here. Matched on `id`
                     alone (the registry's key), so Rendering's frozen re-declaration of a
                     Game-owned skill is satisfied by the `fs-gg-game` row.

And over the CONTENT of a row the manifest also declares (.github#292, epic #266). Checks 1-3 are
predicates over a row's BYTES; check 5 over its PRESENCE. Nothing compared the one field that is
neither -- the emission condition:

  6. PREDICATE MATCHES
                     a row's `materializes-when` equals the declaring manifest's. This is what
                     `skill-union-assert.sh --params` evaluates to decide whether a skill's ABSENCE
                     from a scaffold is legitimate (ADR-0017's `[missing]`/`[unexpected]` classes),
                     so a stale predicate makes the union gate wrong in a direction it cannot
                     detect -- and the digests stay green throughout. It hid `fs-gg-testing` at
                     `profile == governed` for six days after Rendering deliberately widened it to
                     all five profiles (Rendering#90, 2026-07-04), which is exactly the scaffold
                     the registry then claimed should NOT carry a testing skill.

                     Compared on a WHITESPACE-NORMALIZED form (list spacing is not semantics), never
                     on grammar: `profile in [a,b]` == `profile in [a, b]`. It deliberately does NOT
                     normalize the C-style grammar (`(profile == "app" || ...)`) Rendering shipped
                     before Rendering#77. Absorbing that here would make a producer's regression to a
                     grammar the ADR-0017 evaluator CANNOT PARSE -- and therefore reads as false --
                     compare equal and report green: the fails-open shape this epic exists to close.
                     A grammar regression must surface as a divergence. Whether the gate should also
                     reject non-canonical grammar outright is .github#290, not this check.

                     A frozen mirror declares an id twice (ADR-0022 §6); the row's `owner:` picks
                     which manifest is authoritative. If two declarers disagree with EACH OTHER, the
                     row is reported and `--write` refuses to guess.

Producers are DERIVED, never hardcoded: the repos named by `source:` prefixes (which MUST carry a
manifest), plus any other checkout under `--repos-root` that carries one -- so a brand-new producer
whose rows are ALL missing is still read, rather than being invisible for want of a row to name it.

KNOWN BOUND, stated rather than hidden: a producer is recognised by a manifest at one of
MANIFEST_CANDIDATES, directly under its checkout. A repo that publishes its manifest somewhere else
is not seen at all -- and, if the registry names no row from it, is not seen by check 4 either. CI
checks the producers out at exactly these paths, so this is a shape the gate requires, not a hole it
tolerates; a producer that moves its manifest must add the new location here.

Two producers may declare the SAME id -- that is the frozen mirror (ADR-0022 §6), where the copy and
the canonical body are byte-identical. When such an id is MISSING from the registry, which repo owns
it is not derivable from the manifests (the alphabetically first is as likely to be the copy), so the
row is reported and `--write` refuses to guess an `owner:`/`source:` for it.

Digest semantics match `scripts/repos.sh digest`, `Fsgg.SkillMirror.sha256`, and both producer
generators: lowercase hex over the UTF-8 bytes of the body TEXT, with a leading BOM stripped so a
BOM can never enter the digest on either the producing or the verifying side.

Pure-stdlib + PyYAML (already a coherence-gate dependency); no network, no tokens.

Usage:
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check                       # check (default); exit 1 on any finding
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --write               # reconcile in place, exit 0 if clean
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --json                # machine findings
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --repos-root DIR      # where the producer checkouts live
  scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --registry PATH       # explicit skills.yml

Exit: 0 = coherent (or --write left it coherent); 1 = findings / usage error.

`--write` rewrites ONLY the `sha256:` and `materializes-when:` values of a stale row and APPENDS a
row for a skill a manifest declares that the registry lacks, in place, leaving byte-for-byte
everything else (the file is hand-formatted, aligned YAML flow maps -- a full yaml.dump would
destroy it). Both rewrites take the value FROM the manifest -- the digest verbatim, the predicate in
the whitespace-normalized form it is compared in -- which is what "reconciled from the producer
manifests" means; neither invents one. An appended row is
reconciled FROM the manifest entry: `scope`/`sha256`/`materializes-when` verbatim, `source` from the
entry's `supplied-by`, `owner` from the producer repo. A manifest that omits `supplied-by` (SDD's
process manifest does) gives no `source:` to derive, so the row is REPORTED and not appended --
inventing a path would be hand-authoring the bytes the registry header forbids.

Whatever it wrote, `--write` then RE-CHECKS from disk and exits non-zero on anything left, so it can
never report success over a registry that is still incoherent. It does NOT touch `updated:` or
prepend a changelog entry: per the registry protocol those are the operator's, and a reconcile must
be explained, not just applied.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import sys

try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - environment guard
    sys.exit("fsgg-skill-registry-check: PyYAML is required (python -m pip install pyyaml)")

# Rendering keeps frozen copies of the skills FS.GG.Game owns (ADR-0022 §6, retired at P6).
FROZEN_MIRROR_OWNER = "fs-gg-game"
FROZEN_MIRROR_REPO = "FS.GG.Rendering"
FROZEN_MIRROR_PATH = "template/product-skills/{id}/SKILL.md"

# Where a producer publishes its `skill-manifest` (schema v1). SDD EMITS its process manifest into
# the agent-skill root; Rendering and Game publish theirs from the template tree. Probed in order;
# the first hit wins. Locations, not repo names -- the producer SET is derived (see producers()).
MANIFEST_CANDIDATES = (
    ".agents/skills/skill-manifest.json",
    "template/skill-manifest/skill-manifest.json",
)

# ADR-0017: an entry with no `materializes-when` is unconditionally emitted.
DEFAULT_WHEN = "always"

# A canonical-body digest: 64 lowercase hex chars. Anything else is a registry-shape defect.
DIGEST_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{64}")


def canonical_digest(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """sha256 over the body text's UTF-8 bytes, BOM-free (Fsgg.SkillMirror.sha256)."""
    if raw.startswith(b"\xef\xbb\xbf"):
        raw = raw[3:]
    return hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()


def read_bytes(path: str) -> bytes | None:
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            return handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return None


def owner_of(repo: str) -> str:
    """The registry's `owner:` slug for a producer checkout: FS.GG.Rendering -> fs-gg-rendering."""
    return repo.lower().replace(".", "-")


def find_manifest(repos_root: str, repo: str) -> str | None:
    for candidate in MANIFEST_CANDIDATES:
        path = os.path.join(repos_root, repo, candidate)
        if os.path.isfile(path):
            return path
    return None


def producers(repos_root: str, rows: list[dict]) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """(repos that MUST carry a manifest, other checkouts that happen to carry one).

    The first set is derived from the `source:` prefixes -- those repos are the ones the registry
    claims to have been reconciled from, so a manifest missing there is a finding, not a skip.
    The second catches a producer the registry names NOWHERE (every one of its rows missing) --
    which the first set, being keyed on existing rows, is structurally unable to see.
    """
    named = sorted({row["source"].split("/")[0] for row in rows if isinstance(row.get("source"), str)})
    try:
        present = sorted(d for d in os.listdir(repos_root) if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(repos_root, d)))
    except OSError:
        present = []
    unnamed = [r for r in present if r not in named and find_manifest(repos_root, r)]
    return named, unnamed


def read_manifest(path: str) -> tuple[list[dict] | None, str]:
    """(entries, error). A manifest we cannot read or make sense of is a finding, never a skip."""
    try:
        with open(path) as handle:
            doc = json.load(handle)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return None, f"unreadable manifest {path}: {exc}"
    entries = doc.get("skills") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(entries, list):
        return None, f"manifest has no `skills` array: {path}"
    # Guard the ELEMENTS too, not just the array: `{"skills": ["fs-gg-oops"]}` would otherwise
    # reach entry.get() and raise. A manifest we cannot make sense of is a finding, never a crash.
    bad = [i for i, entry in enumerate(entries) if not isinstance(entry, dict)]
    if bad:
        return None, f"manifest `skills[{bad[0]}]` is not an object: {path}"
    return entries, ""


def normalize_when(value: object) -> str:
    """A `materializes-when` in comparable form: whitespace collapsed, list spacing regular.

    Whitespace is not semantics — `profile in [a,b]` and `profile in [a, b]` are the same predicate,
    and a producer reformatting its manifest must not read as drift. GRAMMAR is semantics, and is
    deliberately left alone: see check 6 in the module docstring.

    A double-quoted STRING LITERAL is bytes, not layout: `name == "a,b"` must not become
    `name == "a, b"`. `--write` persists this exact text into the registry, where the union gate
    parses it, so normalizing inside a literal would rewrite the value the gate compares against.
    Literals are stashed behind NUL-delimited placeholders (which carry no space, comma or bracket
    of their own) and restored afterwards.
    """
    literals: list[str] = []

    def stash(match: re.Match) -> str:
        literals.append(match.group(0))
        return f"\x00{len(literals) - 1}\x00"

    text = re.sub(r'"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"', stash, str(value))
    text = " ".join(text.split())
    text = re.sub(r"\s*,\s*", ", ", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\[\s*", "[", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\s*\]", "]", text)
    return re.sub(r"\x00(\d+)\x00", lambda m: literals[int(m.group(1))], text)


def when_of(entry: dict) -> str:
    """A manifest entry's predicate, normalized. Absent ⇒ `always` (ADR-0017)."""
    return normalize_when(entry.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))


def manifest_index(repos_root: str, rows: list[dict]) -> tuple[dict[str, list[dict]], list[dict]]:
    """({skill_id: [{repo, entry}, ...]}, manifest-found findings) over every producer.

    Read ONCE and shared by checks 5 and 6: `declared-completeness` wants the ids this index has
    that the registry lacks, `predicate-matches` the ids they have in common. Records EVERY
    declarer of an id — taking the first would attribute by repo sort order, and the one id two
    producers legitimately share is a frozen mirror (ADR-0022 §6), where the alphabetically-first
    repo is the COPY, not the owner.
    """
    named, unnamed = producers(repos_root, rows)

    findings: list[dict] = []
    declarers: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}

    for repo in named + unnamed:
        manifest_path = find_manifest(repos_root, repo)
        if manifest_path is None:
            # Only reachable for a `named` repo: `unnamed` was filtered on having one.
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": repo,
                    "check": "manifest-found",
                    "detail": (
                        f"{repo} is named by a `source:` prefix but carries no skill-manifest "
                        f"(looked for {', '.join(MANIFEST_CANDIDATES)}) — every skill it declares "
                        "would go unchecked"
                    ),
                }
            )
            continue

        entries, error = read_manifest(manifest_path)
        if entries is None:
            findings.append({"id": repo, "check": "manifest-found", "detail": error})
            continue

        for entry in entries:
            skill_id = entry.get("id")
            if not skill_id:
                continue
            declarers.setdefault(skill_id, []).append({"repo": repo, "entry": entry})

    return declarers, findings


def predicates(rows: list[dict], declarers: dict[str, list[dict]]) -> list[dict]:
    """A row's `materializes-when` must equal its declaring manifest's (.github#292).

    A row NO manifest declares is not this check's business: either its producer's manifest is
    unreadable (check 4 already failed closed on it) or the row names a producer that declares
    nothing — both louder findings than a predicate mismatch would be.
    """
    findings: list[dict] = []

    for row in rows:
        skill_id = row["id"]
        declared_by = declarers.get(skill_id)
        if not declared_by:
            continue

        # The frozen mirror re-declares an id it does not own; `owner:` says whose word is law.
        owned = [d for d in declared_by if owner_of(d["repo"]) == row.get("owner")]
        authoritative = owned or declared_by
        manifest_whens = {when_of(d["entry"]) for d in authoritative}

        if len(manifest_whens) > 1:
            repos = ", ".join(sorted(d["repo"] for d in authoritative))
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "predicate-matches",
                    "detail": (
                        f"its {len(authoritative)} declaring manifests ({repos}) disagree on "
                        f"`materializes-when` ({' vs '.join(sorted(repr(w) for w in manifest_whens))}) "
                        "— --write cannot reconcile a row against producers that contradict each "
                        "other; fix the producers first"
                    ),
                }
            )
            continue

        manifest_when = next(iter(manifest_whens))
        row_when = normalize_when(row.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        if row_when == manifest_when:
            continue

        repo = authoritative[0]["repo"]
        finding = {
            "id": skill_id,
            "check": "predicate-matches",
            "detail": (
                f"registry says `{row_when}` but {repo}'s manifest declares "
                f"`{manifest_when}` — the union gate evaluates this to decide whether the "
                "skill's absence from a scaffold is legitimate"
            ),
            "declared": row_when,
        }
        # `--write` rewrites a VALUE in place; a row that omits the field has none to rewrite, and
        # inserting one would re-align the hand-formatted flow map. Report it instead — `actual` is
        # what marks a finding reconcilable (an absent field reads as `always`, ADR-0017, so this is
        # only reachable when the manifest declares something else).
        if "materializes-when" in row:
            finding["actual"] = manifest_when
        else:
            finding["detail"] += (
                "; --write cannot rewrite it (the row declares no `materializes-when:` field) "
                "— add it by hand"
            )
        findings.append(finding)

    return findings


def completeness(rows: list[dict], declarers: dict[str, list[dict]]) -> list[dict]:
    """Every skill a producer manifest declares must have a row here (.github#289)."""
    declared = {row["id"] for row in rows}

    findings: list[dict] = []
    missing = {i: d for i, d in declarers.items() if i not in declared}

    for skill_id, declared_by in sorted(missing.items()):
        if len(declared_by) > 1:
            repos = ", ".join(d["repo"] for d in declared_by)
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "declared-completeness",
                    "detail": (
                        f"declared by {len(declared_by)} producer manifests ({repos}) but absent from "
                        "the registry; --write cannot append it (which repo is the owner and which "
                        "the frozen mirror is not derivable from the manifests) — add the row by hand"
                    ),
                }
            )
            continue

        repo, entry = declared_by[0]["repo"], declared_by[0]["entry"]
        supplied_by = entry.get("supplied-by")
        when = str(entry.get("materializes-when", DEFAULT_WHEN))
        finding = {
            "id": skill_id,
            "check": "declared-completeness",
            "detail": (
                f"declared by {repo}'s manifest but absent from the registry "
                f"(materializes-when: {when})"
            ),
        }
        # `--write` can only append a row whose `source:` the manifest actually gives us.
        digest = str(entry.get("sha256", ""))
        if supplied_by and DIGEST_RE.fullmatch(digest):
            finding["row"] = {
                "id": skill_id,
                "scope": str(entry.get("scope", "product")),
                "owner": owner_of(repo),
                "source": f"{repo}/{supplied_by.rstrip('/')}/SKILL.md",
                "sha256": digest,
                "materializes-when": when,
            }
        else:
            why = "no `supplied-by`" if not supplied_by else f"unusable sha256 {digest!r}"
            finding["detail"] += f"; --write cannot append it ({why}) — add the row by hand"
        findings.append(finding)

    return findings


def check(registry_path: str, repos_root: str) -> list[dict]:
    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        registry = yaml.safe_load(handle)

    findings: list[dict] = []
    declarers, manifest_findings = manifest_index(repos_root, registry["skills"])

    for row in registry["skills"]:
        skill_id, source = row["id"], row["source"]
        # An unquoted all-digit digest parses as an int, and a truncated one is silently wrong.
        # Both are registry-shape defects, so report rather than crash on them.
        declared = str(row["sha256"])
        if not DIGEST_RE.fullmatch(declared):
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "digest-shape",
                    "detail": f"sha256 is not 64 lowercase hex chars: {declared!r} (quote it in YAML?)",
                }
            )
            continue

        source_path = os.path.join(repos_root, source)

        raw = read_bytes(source_path)
        if raw is None:
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "source-exists",
                    "detail": f"source not found: {source}",
                }
            )
            continue

        actual = canonical_digest(raw)
        if actual != declared:
            findings.append(
                {
                    "id": skill_id,
                    "check": "digest-matches",
                    "detail": f"declared {declared[:8]}… but source hashes to {actual[:8]}…",
                    "declared": declared,
                    "actual": actual,
                }
            )

        if row["owner"] == FROZEN_MIRROR_OWNER:
            mirror = os.path.join(
                repos_root, FROZEN_MIRROR_REPO, FROZEN_MIRROR_PATH.format(id=skill_id)
            )
            mirrored = read_bytes(mirror)
            # Derived, not declared: a row whose frozen copy has been retired simply has none.
            if mirrored is not None and mirrored != raw:
                findings.append(
                    {
                        "id": skill_id,
                        "check": "frozen-mirror",
                        "detail": (
                            f"{FROZEN_MIRROR_REPO}'s frozen copy is not byte-identical to "
                            f"the canonical body ({canonical_digest(mirrored)[:8]}… vs {actual[:8]}…)"
                        ),
                    }
                )

    findings.extend(predicates(registry["skills"], declarers))
    findings.extend(manifest_findings)
    findings.extend(completeness(registry["skills"], declarers))
    return findings


# The separating commas live in the PADDED fields, not the template — a comma in both yields
# `id: x,   ,` and a YAML parse error, which is how the first draft of this failed.
ROW_TEMPLATE = "  - {{ id: {id}scope: {scope}owner: {owner}source: {source} sha256: {sha256}, materializes-when: {when} }}"


def format_when(when: str) -> str:
    """A `materializes-when` as YAML: `always` is a bare token, every real predicate is quoted.

    json.dumps, not f'"{when}"' — a YAML double-quoted scalar escapes exactly as JSON does, so a
    predicate containing a `"` round-trips instead of terminating the scalar early.
    """
    return when if when == DEFAULT_WHEN else json.dumps(when)


def format_row(row: dict) -> str:
    """One hand-aligned YAML flow map, in the shape the surrounding rows are written in."""
    return ROW_TEMPLATE.format(
        id=f"{row['id']},".ljust(24),
        scope=f"{row['scope']},".ljust(9),
        owner=f"{row['owner']},".ljust(17),
        source=f"{row['source']},",
        sha256=row["sha256"],
        when=format_when(row["materializes-when"]),
    )


def append_rows(registry_path: str, findings: list[dict]) -> int:
    """Append a row for each manifest-declared skill the registry lacks, after the last row."""
    rows = [f["row"] for f in findings if f["check"] == "declared-completeness" and "row" in f]
    if not rows:
        return 0

    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        lines = handle.read().splitlines(keepends=True)

    # Anchor on the LAST row rather than EOF: a trailing comment must stay trailing.
    last = max((i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if line.startswith("  - { id:")), default=None)
    if last is None:
        sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: no `- {{ id: ... }}` rows found in {registry_path}")
    if not lines[last].endswith("\n"):
        lines[last] += "\n"

    block = [
        "\n",
        "  # APPENDED by `fsgg-skill-registry-check --write` from the producer manifests (.github#289).\n",
        "  # Reconciled, not hand-authored: scope/sha256/materializes-when verbatim from the manifest\n",
        "  # entry, `source` from its `supplied-by`. Re-home and annotate these into the block they\n",
        "  # belong to, then set `updated:` and prepend a registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry.\n",
    ]
    block += [format_row(row) + "\n" for row in sorted(rows, key=lambda r: r["id"])]

    lines[last + 1 : last + 1] = block
    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write("".join(lines))
    return len(rows)


def reconcile(registry_path: str, findings: list[dict]) -> int:
    """Rewrite only the sha256 of each digest-mismatched row, preserving the hand-aligned YAML."""
    stale = {f["id"]: f["actual"] for f in findings if f["check"] == "digest-matches"}
    if not stale:
        return 0

    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        text = handle.read()

    written = 0
    for skill_id, actual in stale.items():
        # Anchor on the row's own `id:` so we can never rewrite a neighbour's digest.
        pattern = re.compile(
            rf"(\{{\s*id:\s*{re.escape(skill_id)}\s*,[^}}]*?sha256:\s*)([0-9a-f]{{64}})"
        )
        text, count = pattern.subn(rf"\g<1>{actual}", text, count=1)
        if count != 1:
            sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: could not locate the sha256 of row {skill_id}")
        written += 1

    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(text)
    return written


def reconcile_predicates(registry_path: str, findings: list[dict]) -> int:
    """Rewrite only the `materializes-when` of each diverged row, preserving the hand-aligned YAML.

    A row whose declarers contradict each other carries no `actual` and is skipped — `--write`'s
    re-check then leaves it as the finding it is, rather than picking a producer to believe.
    """
    stale = {f["id"]: f["actual"] for f in findings if f["check"] == "predicate-matches" and "actual" in f}
    if not stale:
        return 0

    with open(registry_path) as handle:
        text = handle.read()

    written = 0
    for skill_id, actual in stale.items():
        # Anchor on the row's own `id:`, as reconcile() does. `[^}]` cannot cross the row's closing
        # brace, so this can never rewrite a neighbour's predicate; and the `\s*,` after the id keeps
        # `fs-gg-scene` from matching a `fs-gg-scene-extra` row.
        # ASSUMES, as every row in this file holds: `materializes-when` is the row's LAST field, and
        # no predicate contains a `}`. Both are true of the ADR-0017 grammar (bare tokens and
        # bracketed lists); a predicate carrying `}` would slice the value group early, so a future
        # grammar that admits one must anchor this on the field order instead.
        pattern = re.compile(
            rf"(\{{\s*id:\s*{re.escape(skill_id)}\s*,[^}}]*?materializes-when:\s*)([^}}]*?)(\s*\}})"
        )
        # A function replacement, not a template: `format_when` emits a JSON scalar, whose
        # backslashes would otherwise be read as group references.
        text, count = pattern.subn(
            lambda m, value=actual: f"{m.group(1)}{format_when(value)}{m.group(3)}", text, count=1
        )
        if count != 1:
            sys.exit(f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: could not locate the materializes-when of row {skill_id}")
        written += 1

    with open(registry_path, "w") as handle:
        handle.write(text)
    return written


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True, description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("--registry", default="registry/skills.yml")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--repos-root",
        default=".repos",
        help="directory holding the producer checkouts (FS.GG.SDD/, FS.GG.Rendering/, FS.GG.Game/)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="emit findings as JSON")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--write", action="store_true", help="reconcile stale sha256 / materializes-when in place"
    )
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if not os.path.isdir(args.repos_root):
        sys.exit(
            f"fsgg-skill-registry-check: --repos-root '{args.repos_root}' is not a directory. "
            "Check out the producer repos there (see .github/workflows/skill-registry-coherence.yml)."
        )

    findings = check(args.registry, args.repos_root)

    if args.write:
        appended = append_rows(args.registry, findings)
        written = reconcile(args.registry, findings)
        repredicated = reconcile_predicates(args.registry, findings)
        print(
            f"skill-registry: reconciled {written} sha256 row(s) and {repredicated} "
            f"materializes-when value(s), appended {appended} missing row(s) in {args.registry}"
        )
        # Re-check from disk. --write cannot invent a missing source, resolve a mirror divergence,
        # append a row the manifest gave no `supplied-by` for, or pick a side when two producers
        # declare contradictory predicates — and an appended row whose manifest digest disagrees
        # with the body surfaces here rather than on someone else's CI.
        blocked = check(args.registry, args.repos_root)
        if blocked:
            for finding in blocked:
                print(f"  still unresolved [{finding['check']}] {finding['id']}: {finding['detail']}")
            return 1
        print("  remember: set `updated:` and prepend a registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry")
        return 0

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps({"findings": findings}, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
    elif findings:
        print(f"skill-registry: {len(findings)} finding(s) — registry ≠ manifest ≠ bytes")
        for finding in findings:
            print(f"  [{finding['check']}] {finding['id']}: {finding['detail']}")
        print(
            "\nskills.yml is reconciled FROM the producer manifests (ADR-0017), never hand-authored.\n"
            "Fix: scripts/fsgg-skill-registry-check --write --repos-root <dir>, then set `updated:`\n"
            "and prepend a dated registry/skills.CHANGELOG.md entry naming the producer PR."
        )
    else:
        print(f"skill-registry: coherent — registry = manifest = bytes")

    return 1 if findings else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
